WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:00.943
- Two things happened.

00:00:00.943 --> 00:00:03.410
I was faster and it cost me less.

00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:06.250
I was faster because when
Opus one or Château Lafitte

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:08.930
or Dom Perignon's new vintage came out,

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:11.470
I would email it the day it came in

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:12.900
and I would get the sale.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.620
A month later,

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.420
those individuals would get the catalog

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:20.160
from my competitors saying
the Dom Perignon's here.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.370
So I was winning on speed but also cost.

00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:25.730
And then finally, the tipping
point was Google AdWords.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:29.420
I was smart enough to realize
Google was a big platform,

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:31.580
they launched the AdWords product

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:34.070
and literally on the day they
launched the AdWords product,

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:36.320
I started buying wine terms.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.230
At first wine, Bordeaux, Mirlo, Champagne,

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.640
later I had to pick, you
know, have to get smarter,

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:43.890
because people started figuring it out.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:47.430
But I had about a year there
where I was paying 5 cents

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:49.027
and then it became 10 cents

00:00:49.027 --> 00:00:51.030
per every wine term you can imagine

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.993
and acquiring customers for nothing.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.470
Sometimes 20, 30 cents to get customers,

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.770
active customers, which is insane.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.950
So that became, about a couple years ago,

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.490
I realized what I'd been talking about

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:05.010
was underpriced attention.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:08.910
Meaning, the reason I'm so
passionate about LinkedIn now,

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:11.690
but didn't say a word
about it 6 years ago,

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:14.730
is the organic reach on a LinkedIn post

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:17.100
for a business right now is meaningful.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.220
Because LinkedIn has so
much attention on it,

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.720
yet not as much content and ads on it yet.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.520
Because this is what always happens.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.320
You're able to get some
organic reach when you post.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.020
To me that is the holy grail of marketing,

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:32.853
when you're getting people

00:01:32.853 --> 00:01:34.890
to know about your business or you

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:38.940
and it costs you less aka
either 0 on organic reach,

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:43.940
or why Facebook became so
big when you can spend $50

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.240
and get people to see it.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.698
(upbeat music)

00:01:47.698 --> 00:01:49.083
You got your perspective.

00:01:49.083 --> 00:01:51.564
(upbeat music)

00:01:51.564 --> 00:01:52.530
(audience applauds)

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:53.363
Don't you want to be happy,

00:01:53.363 --> 00:01:54.397
don't you wanna be happy?

00:01:54.397 --> 00:01:57.810
(upbeat music)

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:58.850
- Hi, everyone.

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:00.508
Thanks for joining us this afternoon.

00:02:00.508 --> 00:02:02.590
We're delighted to be
hosting Gary Vaynerchuk

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:03.860
for the next hour.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.240
We will be having Q&amp;A at the end,

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.350
so please make sure you get your questions

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:10.700
in the right hand corner
so we can get to them.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.829
So this guy needs no introduction.

00:02:13.829 --> 00:02:16.813
Gary is the chairman of VaynerX,

00:02:16.813 --> 00:02:21.397
the active CEO of
VaynerMedia, CEO VaynerSports,

00:02:22.488 --> 00:02:25.110
serial entrepreneur and
prolific angel investor

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.170
and five times New York Best Seller.

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:28.950
So welcome, Gary.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:30.859
- Thank you so much, Emma, how're you?

00:02:30.859 --> 00:02:33.130
- I'm good, How're you keeping?

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:35.330
- I'm keeping, it's been,

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:37.600
I think all of us will very much struggle

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.056
to ever forget 2020.

00:02:40.056 --> 00:02:42.340
And I'm really excited to be here,

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:45.530
I just want to say hello to
everybody on the LinkedIn side,

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:48.030
a platform I'm extremely fond of

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:51.800
and I've had the luxury
of doing events in America

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:55.253
and other parts and big
special shout out to Niall,

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:59.710
who worked with me at Vayner
for many, many, many years,

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:02.220
who I know is having a
fantastic career on LinkedIn.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:04.020
And I've not been doing
a whole lot of these

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.460
because I've been really
in operations mode.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.410
But when he asked, I
knew I had to say yes.

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:11.070
So I'm really glad to be here with you.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:12.290
- Oh, that's great. Thanks so much

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:14.380
and I know we've loads
of LinkedIn attendees

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:15.940
who are so excited to get your insight

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:17.473
and perspective on the industry.

00:03:17.473 --> 00:03:19.940
So I'll asked we dive
in with some questions.

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:21.410
But the first thing I have to ask you

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:23.210
and your LinkedIn style

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:25.400
and it wouldn't be a
LinkedIn event without it is,

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.023
can you tell us what's not
on your LinkedIn profile?

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.880
- What's not on my LinkedIn.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:34.054
So when you're a prolific entrepreneur,

00:03:34.054 --> 00:03:39.054
you end up forgetting some
other things you're even doing.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:41.690
So I've had, for example,

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:43.210
what's not on my LinkedIn profile.

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:45.110
In the last 36 months,

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:48.420
I've had 2 companies that
I've co-founded or founded.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:52.120
Empathy Wines, which was a
direct to consumer wine brand

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:53.490
and Resy, which was

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:58.000
a restaurant app POS
system for the restaurants

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.490
and on the consumer side, demand side,

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:03.650
have substantial exits Resy sold

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:06.240
for hefty 9 figures to Amex

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:08.656
and Empathy. I just sold about a month

00:04:08.656 --> 00:04:10.840
and a half ago to Constellation Brands,

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:13.170
the biggest wine company in the world,

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:15.820
for almost $100 million exit.

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:19.400
And neither of those 2
substantial business wins

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:21.350
are on my LinkedIn profile.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:22.930
So I would say probably, you know,

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:24.770
a lot of times when people see me,

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:27.640
they think of me as a
motivational speaker,

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.120
as a content creator.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.860
And a lot of times if I'm
being very transparent,

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.970
a part of me is upset by that

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:37.170
because I have so much
pride in the fact that,

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:38.634
between building my dad's business,

00:04:38.634 --> 00:04:43.634
building Vayner, Resy,
Empathy, my angel investments,

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:46.580
I have a substantial actual career

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:51.020
that's overshadowed by my
prolific content creation.

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:53.040
And sometimes that's
my own fault and doing

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.910
because you're I have
these big business wins

00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:59.820
and I'm not even listing
them on my LinkedIn account.

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.520
So as soon as I'm hanging up here,

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.210
I'm gonna get to work and
make sure it gets updated.

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:06.730
- You should, you should,
they're amazing achievements.

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:09.830
And you talk about that,
your personal brand.

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:10.880
I mean, There's no doubt it.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.094
You've been a pioneer in the practice of

00:05:13.094 --> 00:05:15.350
building a personal brand.

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:18.293
How do you feel it's contributed
to your to your success?

00:05:19.868 --> 00:05:22.553
- When you put yourself
out there as a human being,

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:25.950
there's no question to me that

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:27.740
there's an incredible amount of benefits.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:31.760
I started an agency, an
advertising agency 11 years ago,

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:32.750
really 9 years ago,

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:34.900
because the first 2 years
I was quite passive,

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:36.303
my younger brother really held it down,

00:05:36.303 --> 00:05:38.490
because I was still
running my dad's business.

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:39.720
And it was actually,

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:41.830
what really threw me off
was I wrote my first book,

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:42.663
Crush It.

00:05:42.663 --> 00:05:46.510
And that became a New York
Times best seller and a big one.

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:49.890
And that kind of got
me, pulled into speaking

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:51.780
and other things, and I wanted
to take advantage of that.

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:54.610
So, but I started this business

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:58.260
coming from being a wine
merchant in New Jersey.

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:02.470
So, it was really my personal
brand, the book, the speeches,

00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:05.930
the content that even gave us a chance

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:08.940
to have those first
conversations with the Pepsi,

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:12.060
with the Campbells, with the GE.

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:14.420
And it was built on that
back, because otherwise,

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:17.530
there was no knowledge,
there was no rationale

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:18.770
for the biggest companies in the world

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:21.970
to take a meeting with us,
started by a wine merchant

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:24.320
and a kid that came out of college.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:29.320
So it's contributed in the start
of Vayner, for Sure, Media.

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:33.690
It's contributed in the fact that

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:38.170
Mark Zuckerberg allowed me
to invest in his company,

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:42.370
because of my personal brand,
not because of anything else.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:44.700
I was just, again, a wine merchant,

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:47.050
and now Twitter and Facebook and Tumblr,

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:50.430
David Karp playing cook at Twitter.

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:52.880
These humans were interested in me

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:56.040
because of the content I was
putting out on the internet.

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:58.760
And it became the gateway,

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:03.730
the ticket to let me into
different places in my life.

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:06.953
So, it's been a huge door opener.

00:07:08.919 --> 00:07:10.620
When you put out content to the world,

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:13.350
what happens next is
people reach out to you.

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:17.240
So, whether it's Dwayne The Rock Johnson

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.990
or whether it's Johnson and Johnson,

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:22.010
I have had people reach out to me

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:23.680
instead of me having to reach out to them

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:25.230
because I've created brand

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:30.360
and that's been incredibly
powerful to my career.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.960
The The downside is you have to live

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:34.800
and die by your personality.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:37.590
So I am a scrappy immigrant kid

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:40.240
that grew up in middle class New Jersey.

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:43.950
And so that has meant that
my content has cursing in it,

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:47.650
which, you know, So I've just
told you some nice things,

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:50.450
I know for a fact that
we were on the verge

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:52.430
of getting a multi million dollar contract

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:54.520
from a fortune 50 company

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:58.820
and a board member saw
LinkedIn, actually this is funny

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:01.330
to tell you guys, saw a LinkedIn video,

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:04.020
where I was crass in my language,

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:06.020
not in what I was talking about

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:09.610
and literally sent an email to the CEO,

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:12.680
which the CEO then sent an
email to the division leader

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:14.530
that basically eliminated our agency

00:08:14.530 --> 00:08:15.670
from being able to do the work

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:18.760
because she was unhappy with that.

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:23.760
And I'm competitive and I'm
an over the top personality.

00:08:24.684 --> 00:08:29.684
So there's definitely been
some losses along the way.

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:35.240
But you kind of live
your life in knowing that

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:37.860
there's some pluses and minuses to it.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:39.360
When you put yourself out there,

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:42.400
you've allowed people
to make judgment of you.

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:44.860
And though 98% of the comments

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:46.970
on every LinkedIn posts that I put out,

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:48.600
are incredibly positive,

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:51.010
there's no question that there's 2% of,

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:52.750
well, "This guy's a charlatan,

00:08:52.750 --> 00:08:57.230
he actually doesn't know
anything, da, da, da, da, da, da"

00:08:57.230 --> 00:08:58.960
And when you're a human being,

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:01.060
no matter how thick your skin is,

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:03.340
no matter how much success you have,

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:06.157
it never feels nice to have somebody

00:09:06.157 --> 00:09:08.960
call you names or degrade you.

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:13.550
And so it's a process
that is not for everyone.

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:15.510
But I do believe that net has

00:09:15.510 --> 00:09:18.983
more positive than negative impacts.

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:24.011
- Yeah, that's really
interesting I mean...

00:09:24.011 --> 00:09:25.580
- The reason that's it's interesting

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:27.750
and I appreciate kind of the
way that processed for you

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:32.400
and I hope for the people
that are watching is

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.360
it's the reason most
people don't do it, Emma.

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:37.980
The reason most people
don't put out content,

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:41.420
like to me again, you know
what's fun about living my life?

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:44.500
I put out all my content all the time.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:47.090
So I think everybody
here knows that I'm not

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:49.760
pandering to the LinkedIn
audience right now.

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:51.586
I have been an incredibly big advocate

00:09:51.586 --> 00:09:55.110
about LinkedIn content
for the last 5 years.

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:58.630
It's a game changer for
people's professional careers.

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:01.210
LinkedIn's organic reach is remarkable,

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:03.520
best in the world along with TikTok,

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:07.460
meaning anybody here, Gavin, you,

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:09.330
Jennifer Shaw Sweet, Christopher Glen,

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:12.870
anybody here tomorrow can
make their first video

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:16.460
on their LinkedIn account
about something maybe different

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:19.633
and start the process of having
more people know about them.

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:22.180
But the reason most people don't is

00:10:22.180 --> 00:10:24.330
they can't deal with
the negative pushback.

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:28.310
And that is unfortunate.

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:30.870
And that's why I've spent a
lot of time with my content,

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:34.410
talking about self esteem
and self worth and self love

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:37.760
and patience and all the things
that are needed and required

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:39.250
to actually have a chance to be

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:41.730
successful at content creation.

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:46.730
It's much more of a mental
game than it is a creative.

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:48.580
What do I post?

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:51.423
Can I deal with the
ramifications of posting?

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:54.210
- All right, yeah.

00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:58.030
you talk about how you're
using LinkedIn now,

00:10:58.030 --> 00:11:00.970
like how have you seen
LinkedIn evolve as a platform,

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:03.610
and how are you using it now versus say,

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:04.560
a couple of years ago,

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:06.440
and potentially, how
are your teams using it

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:08.090
as well for business development?

00:11:10.030 --> 00:11:12.460
- I would argue, and I give Facebook

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:13.920
an incredible amount of credit

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:15.830
for their evolution
over the last 15 years,

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:17.760
but I would still say that out

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:19.550
of all the platforms in the world,

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:20.460
the one that has gone through

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:23.850
the most significant
transformation is LinkedIn.

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:24.800
Here's why.

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:27.523
It started off slowly as a utility.

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:31.920
There was no part of LinkedIn 7 years ago

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:34.980
that made people think
content creation, social,

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:38.080
I mean people refer to
LinkedIn as a social network,

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:40.790
now, in certain pockets of the industry.

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:44.500
So 6 years ago,

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:46.373
I could go an entire year
without going to my LinkedIn,

00:11:46.373 --> 00:11:50.510
because I was an entrepreneur,
I wasn't looking for jobs.

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:53.520
And the end, pretty much.

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:57.490
Now, I would argue that when
I have a business objective,

00:11:57.490 --> 00:11:59.810
it's the first place I think about.

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:04.460
And so it's also evolved into VaynerMedia,

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:07.980
we produce an enormous amount of

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:11.843
broad content with other CMOs,
not even our own clients.

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:14.420
We do, we're doing something called

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:16.210
Marketing For the Now Right Now,

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:20.440
which is a 2 hour free
seminar once every 10 days,

00:12:20.440 --> 00:12:22.810
where we interview a bunch
of the thought leaders

00:12:22.810 --> 00:12:23.960
in the business world.

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:26.920
And I would argue that 50%
of the reason we do it is

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:28.569
just to give us post production creative

00:12:28.569 --> 00:12:31.060
for our VaynerMedia, LinkedIn,

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:33.200
because that brings awareness

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:35.610
from other marketers about our agency.

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:38.630
And so it has become a massive force,

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:40.420
and is also a big part of our playbook

00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:42.080
as an agency provider.

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:46.560
We we have become very aggressive
going after B2B clients,

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:49.060
because we think instead
of doing print ads

00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:50.210
in the B2B magazine,

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:53.530
or buying million dollar
booths at conferences,

00:12:53.530 --> 00:12:55.170
that LinkedIn content strategy

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:58.900
is the single best strategy
for every B2B marketer.

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:01.180
And now, even scarier,

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:05.150
last night I was talking
to a food CPG company.

00:13:05.150 --> 00:13:06.437
And during the meeting, I said,

00:13:06.437 --> 00:13:08.020
"Look, I think LinkedIn is something

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:08.853
you need to think about,

00:13:08.853 --> 00:13:12.100
I think LinkedIn is acting
like Facebook did 7 years ago.

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:13.790
And I know how you're thinking about it,

00:13:13.790 --> 00:13:16.400
senior marketer who's not in the trenches,

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:17.380
but I would tell you that

00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:20.910
if you put out recipe content
on LinkedIn right now,

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:23.490
it will do extremely well.

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:28.300
And you should be looking at
this platform a different way."

00:13:28.300 --> 00:13:32.423
And so I think people are
starting to kind of "Get it"

00:13:32.423 --> 00:13:34.623
that there's something
really going on here.

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:38.503
- You talk like this marketing for

00:13:38.503 --> 00:13:41.000
a now initiative that you're running.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:42.060
How do you see like,

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:44.890
with everything that's happened
this year and a pandemic,

00:13:44.890 --> 00:13:48.041
How do you see recovery
taking shape for marketeers?

00:13:48.041 --> 00:13:49.674
How do you see them coming out of this?

00:13:49.674 --> 00:13:51.410
What's your perspective on this?

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:53.140
- My perspective is
that the global economy

00:13:53.140 --> 00:13:56.410
is in much worse shape than
anyone's really talking about,

00:13:56.410 --> 00:13:58.860
because everyone's printed money.

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:01.330
We've got a lot of conflict,

00:14:01.330 --> 00:14:04.000
you've got nuances of nationalism

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.210
sprinting everywhere around the world

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:09.010
led by America and China.

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:12.200
And I think I would
argue that, first of all,

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:14.720
I have no clue because
we're not out of it, right?

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:16.920
What's really difficult
about that question,

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:20.623
when I'm asked it is, look, first,

00:14:21.740 --> 00:14:25.480
geopolitical and global economic
variables have to play out

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:27.397
and that's a little
bit above my pay grade.

00:14:27.397 --> 00:14:28.980
I haven't done that homework.

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:30.690
But what I can tell you is,

00:14:30.690 --> 00:14:33.430
it's very clear to me that
the common sense fundamentals

00:14:33.430 --> 00:14:34.850
are in a very vulnerable spot

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:36.930
and you have a major election in the US

00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:38.990
and you have some pretty aggressive things

00:14:38.990 --> 00:14:40.010
going around the world.

00:14:40.010 --> 00:14:44.290
You have conflict in my former
birthplace of Belarus where

00:14:44.290 --> 00:14:46.130
they're trying to overthrow a dictator.

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:49.080
You've got real "stuff" going on.

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:54.080
And so I think that I think
that people are a little naive,

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:56.670
in thinking, well,

00:14:56.670 --> 00:14:59.333
I think the world has had a very good run,

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:02.090
if you think about it economically,

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:06.690
outside of the recession,
the global recession of 2008.

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:09.220
The reality is that the world has had

00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:13.520
a fairly good run economically
over the last 40 years.

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:16.240
And I think that that
has made us very soft

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:19.000
and very naive to what could happen.

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:20.780
Now, I'm not predicting doomsday

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:22.630
or depressions or great recessions.

00:15:22.630 --> 00:15:26.550
But I will say that we are so far away

00:15:26.550 --> 00:15:27.950
from understanding recovery,

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:30.370
because recovery is a fascinating thing.

00:15:30.370 --> 00:15:32.920
This is not like a war or a recession,

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:36.140
where you've already
understood the dynamics.

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:37.420
One could argue that

00:15:37.420 --> 00:15:40.827
you will never see human
beings again at an airport,

00:15:40.827 --> 00:15:43.070
not wearing a mask.

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:44.760
Let's just say that's true, Emma.

00:15:44.760 --> 00:15:48.120
If that's true, just on that,

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:50.770
just on "everything went back to normal,"

00:15:50.770 --> 00:15:54.421
except whenever humans
travel, they were a mask.

00:15:54.421 --> 00:15:58.293
Just that heavily impacts
a lot of businesses,

00:15:59.130 --> 00:16:03.850
Bravo, food and beverage
businesses that rely on

00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:06.420
the ecosystems of global airports,

00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:09.260
there's just a, duty free shops,

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:10.547
like, there's just that little

00:16:10.547 --> 00:16:13.090
and by the way, all of us know
that, that's not the case.

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:15.397
There'll be many, many
more ramifications of this.

00:16:15.397 --> 00:16:18.860
And so not to mention something that

00:16:18.860 --> 00:16:20.174
I don't think people remember about.

00:16:20.174 --> 00:16:22.680
In America, I talk to a
lot of executives about

00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:25.390
remember 911, it was devastating.

00:16:25.390 --> 00:16:27.390
And then for the next 5 years,

00:16:27.390 --> 00:16:30.780
anytime anything happened with a plane

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:32.880
or this, you were gun shy.

00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:37.880
I mean, I expect every human
being for the next 5 years

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:41.540
to overreact to another human being

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:44.113
sneezing in any situation.

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:46.673
What does that mean?

00:16:46.673 --> 00:16:48.260
Just think about that,

00:16:48.260 --> 00:16:51.220
does that mean that events

00:16:51.220 --> 00:16:54.480
and restaurants have a 3% decline?

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:56.023
Let's just say 3%, Emma.

00:16:57.210 --> 00:16:59.430
Restaurants work on such low margin

00:16:59.430 --> 00:17:02.460
that if they just lose
3-5% of their business

00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:07.460
because the most extreme of
us are scared post Corona

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:09.690
and really kind of just

00:17:09.690 --> 00:17:11.940
don't leave the house anymore period.

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:13.330
Or far less often,

00:17:13.330 --> 00:17:16.490
if you were somebody who went
out to eat 20 times a year,

00:17:16.490 --> 00:17:17.603
you're down to 3.

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:20.300
If that's the case,

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:23.120
for some businesses they can't
recover with that 5% loss,

00:17:23.120 --> 00:17:24.420
that was all their profit.

00:17:25.490 --> 00:17:28.760
And so I think the
ramifications are substantial.

00:17:28.760 --> 00:17:30.660
On the flip side,

00:17:30.660 --> 00:17:32.930
there's a lot of businesses
that will explode.

00:17:32.930 --> 00:17:35.790
Every direct to consumer business,

00:17:35.790 --> 00:17:39.410
on the internet is going to
grow with that transition.

00:17:39.410 --> 00:17:42.400
So I think there's still a lot unknown.

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:47.400
As a matter of fact this
morning back to the UK market,

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:50.870
this morning, I woke up to
Boris Johnson saying that

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:52.920
they're gonna take the groups,

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:55.493
that gatherings down from 30 to 6.

00:17:56.910 --> 00:17:59.360
That's a step backwards
to recovery, right?

00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:00.450
That doesn't mean,

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:03.540
and I think that this is gonna
go on for a little while,

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:05.130
and I don't think people really,

00:18:05.130 --> 00:18:06.970
I don't think most people realize

00:18:06.970 --> 00:18:09.630
that it's going to be next summer,

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:12.720
if everything goes remarkably well,

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:15.900
to where you kind of might
start seeing the first

00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:18.835
toe in the water back to normalcy.

00:18:18.835 --> 00:18:20.263
That's a long time.

00:18:20.263 --> 00:18:23.180
I remember when this all happened.

00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:24.750
I thought, oh my God!

00:18:24.750 --> 00:18:28.440
We're gonna close the offices
for 2 weeks, that's crazy.

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:30.910
It's gonna end up being closer
to 2 years than 2 weeks.

00:18:30.910 --> 00:18:34.001
So I think that, I think
it's unprecedented times

00:18:34.001 --> 00:18:36.840
and I think it's way early
to figure out what happens.

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:39.260
But what I can tell you is for me

00:18:39.260 --> 00:18:42.170
and this is just, I would call this luck.

00:18:42.170 --> 00:18:45.440
For me, the fact that
everything in my mind has been

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:49.190
about video streaming, and
the cell phone and you know,

00:18:49.190 --> 00:18:51.600
lack of friction, internet only.

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:54.650
The real world is
secondary, nice important,

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:56.933
but it's secondary to this world.

00:18:57.840 --> 00:19:00.682
That has put me in a
position as a communicator

00:19:00.682 --> 00:19:04.100
and as a business
operator in a better spot

00:19:04.100 --> 00:19:07.250
than some businesses because
I bet on the internet

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:11.540
and the internet is the
real winner of this reality.

00:19:14.060 --> 00:19:19.060
I would very likely be doing
this in person with you, Emma,

00:19:19.150 --> 00:19:21.570
the next time I was in the region.

00:19:21.570 --> 00:19:24.820
Now, I'll be able to do 3 times with you,

00:19:24.820 --> 00:19:26.650
and I'm sitting in my apartment right now.

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:29.660
And so, I can tell you right now,

00:19:29.660 --> 00:19:32.240
I think it takes generations

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:35.390
for the airline and travel
industry to recover from this.

00:19:35.390 --> 00:19:39.600
Because even when everybody
in 5 years is back to normal,

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:41.050
I'm still going to debate,

00:19:41.050 --> 00:19:44.050
do I need to go to San
Francisco today for this meeting

00:19:44.050 --> 00:19:48.130
Or can I do this on zoom
or hangouts or BlueJeans?

00:19:48.130 --> 00:19:51.113
And the answer 6/10 times
is I can do it on BlueJeans.

00:19:54.202 --> 00:19:55.240
(Emma inaudible)

00:19:55.240 --> 00:19:57.120
- It's the changes that
are going to happen

00:19:57.120 --> 00:19:58.440
for all these industries you name

00:19:58.440 --> 00:19:59.635
so some are going to explode

00:19:59.635 --> 00:20:02.770
and some are gonna be
devastated for years to come.

00:20:02.770 --> 00:20:05.340
I guess it's, as sales people ourselves,

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:08.200
what kind of advice would
you have for us approaching

00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:10.590
these marketeer guys, these CMOs, or CEOs,

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:13.200
whether they're of advertising
agencies like yourselves

00:20:13.200 --> 00:20:14.480
or if they're direct for clients?

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:16.263
What advice would you give us?

00:20:17.120 --> 00:20:20.040
- Let me go very narrow with this answer.

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:22.890
Let's make pretend the
pandemic didn't even happen.

00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:26.150
I would tell everybody
here to have more pride

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:28.411
in the product you're selling.

00:20:28.411 --> 00:20:30.320
I have the unfortunate thing

00:20:30.320 --> 00:20:32.340
where I get to do fireside
chats with companies

00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:34.030
that I think are going through challenges.

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:36.230
And because I'm, this is being recorded,

00:20:36.230 --> 00:20:38.230
I want to be historically correct.

00:20:38.230 --> 00:20:39.750
So I'm a nice person,

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:42.310
but I don't want to say
something I don't believe.

00:20:42.310 --> 00:20:45.357
So sometimes, when that
questions asked, I get into,

00:20:45.357 --> 00:20:47.940
"Hey, can I speak to the
engineers and the product people

00:20:47.940 --> 00:20:50.000
because your products
not very good right now

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:51.860
and who cares if you're a good salesman,

00:20:51.860 --> 00:20:54.670
you're not gonna have
good lifetime value."

00:20:54.670 --> 00:20:56.770
I think I have the great fortune

00:20:56.770 --> 00:20:58.470
to have the reverse conversation right now

00:20:58.470 --> 00:21:00.230
with everybody on this call.

00:21:00.230 --> 00:21:01.675
I would tell you the number one thing

00:21:01.675 --> 00:21:03.490
that I would tell everybody here is

00:21:03.490 --> 00:21:06.800
to have way more pride
in what you're selling.

00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:09.570
I know, because I'm
very close to the world,

00:21:09.570 --> 00:21:12.500
that most brands, B2B included,

00:21:12.500 --> 00:21:15.880
have not accepted the incredible
power that LinkedIn is

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:18.470
as a content and marketing engine.

00:21:18.470 --> 00:21:20.950
It is everyone's job on this call

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:25.750
to actually break through
to agencies and to brands.

00:21:25.750 --> 00:21:28.720
And I think that most of
you will be too passive.

00:21:28.720 --> 00:21:31.140
I think you need to go in
with way more conviction,

00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:33.490
way more audacity because

00:21:33.490 --> 00:21:35.571
you actually do have one of the best tools

00:21:35.571 --> 00:21:38.750
in marketing at your fingertips

00:21:38.750 --> 00:21:40.460
and I know the world doesn't know it.

00:21:40.460 --> 00:21:44.040
So you need to cross all your
T's and I's with the examples.

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:46.920
But most of all, you need
to go into those rooms

00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:50.850
on the borderline of audacious,

00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:53.250
because you're selling
one of the best products.

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:56.650
And I know that's not the tone and tenor.

00:21:56.650 --> 00:21:57.870
Because what happens is,

00:21:57.870 --> 00:22:00.370
when something's not
accepted by the world,

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:03.063
most sales people come in too passive.

00:22:04.320 --> 00:22:05.760
And my career has been,

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:08.476
when I know I have the right thing,

00:22:08.476 --> 00:22:10.930
when "it's right" for the customer,

00:22:10.930 --> 00:22:12.830
then if I'm going to somebody in between

00:22:12.830 --> 00:22:17.250
the humans that use it and
like there's a decision maker,

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:22.250
aka the CEO of Wieden+Kennedy,
or the CMO of Cadbury.

00:22:25.030 --> 00:22:26.580
If that person's in between

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:29.020
that company doing it with LinkedIn,

00:22:29.020 --> 00:22:30.430
and the people that actually consume it,

00:22:30.430 --> 00:22:32.570
the actual people in
the field of LinkedIn.

00:22:32.570 --> 00:22:34.340
Well and I know LinkedIn is right,

00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:36.740
well, I'm being very aggressive.

00:22:36.740 --> 00:22:39.450
In my conviction, I'm very convicted.

00:22:39.450 --> 00:22:42.320
And I think clients and agencies

00:22:42.320 --> 00:22:44.760
need to hear that from a salesperson,

00:22:44.760 --> 00:22:47.960
because if they're skeptical
and you come in too passive,

00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:50.023
you're too far away now to move them.

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:53.590
And so, as a salesperson,

00:22:53.590 --> 00:22:56.010
I've only sold things I believe in,

00:22:56.010 --> 00:22:58.570
because I don't know how to
be a salesperson when I don't,

00:22:58.570 --> 00:23:00.670
because it requires too much conviction

00:23:00.670 --> 00:23:01.920
to get somebody on board.

00:23:04.640 --> 00:23:07.380
Listen, this is a real
pleasure and joy for me.

00:23:07.380 --> 00:23:10.630
I hope everybody on this
call really realizes

00:23:10.630 --> 00:23:12.713
how strong the product is right now.

00:23:13.640 --> 00:23:15.560
The organic reach is remarkable

00:23:15.560 --> 00:23:18.080
and the targeting is remarkable.

00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:20.670
Because it's not replicated
anywhere else in the world,

00:23:20.670 --> 00:23:24.740
I cannot get to that many
business decision makers

00:23:24.740 --> 00:23:28.700
all with the same title
on their job description

00:23:28.700 --> 00:23:30.360
anywhere else in the world.

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:32.770
That is the ultimate weapon.

00:23:32.770 --> 00:23:35.180
And the same way that it
took Facebook a half a decade

00:23:35.180 --> 00:23:38.160
to get its feet under them
to really go out there

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:39.900
and get the dollars.

00:23:39.900 --> 00:23:41.173
I think that we're,

00:23:42.350 --> 00:23:43.660
I'm hoping for your sake.

00:23:43.660 --> 00:23:45.870
I mean to me in a weird way,

00:23:45.870 --> 00:23:47.660
to me, the longer people don't know

00:23:47.660 --> 00:23:50.470
that LinkedIn is this strong
is better for Vayner and me

00:23:50.470 --> 00:23:52.960
because I'm picking up more organic reach

00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:57.960
but for the context of what
I was invited to speak here,

00:23:58.170 --> 00:24:01.550
this is a rare opportunity
for me to flex on your behalf.

00:24:01.550 --> 00:24:05.477
This is a very great era
for LinkedIn marketing

00:24:05.477 --> 00:24:08.883
and I hope that you're
communicating that to your clients.

00:24:10.130 --> 00:24:12.293
- Glad to hear that, thank
you for that feedback.

00:24:13.160 --> 00:24:15.420
You were talking about organic reach.

00:24:15.420 --> 00:24:18.830
And you talk quite a bit
about underpriced attention.

00:24:18.830 --> 00:24:19.940
Can you talk a little bit

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:21.998
of what you mean by underpriced attention

00:24:21.998 --> 00:24:23.798
and how can LinkedIn lean into that?

00:24:25.360 --> 00:24:27.900
- Oh, underpriced attention,
I've come to realize,

00:24:27.900 --> 00:24:28.935
is really my religion.

00:24:28.935 --> 00:24:32.070
I built my dad's business
from a $3.8 million business

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:36.250
to a $65 million business in
7 years and I had no money.

00:24:36.250 --> 00:24:39.360
And the reason I was able
to do that was 3 things.

00:24:39.360 --> 00:24:42.010
1. I realized that having a website

00:24:42.010 --> 00:24:43.320
was going to be important.

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:46.200
So I built a website and
that alone was a big deal.

00:24:46.200 --> 00:24:48.000
This is 1996.

00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:51.080
Number 2. A year later,

00:24:51.080 --> 00:24:53.120
I realized email was a big deal.

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:55.508
And this was the great gift of my career.

00:24:55.508 --> 00:25:00.010
Email, ended up being a
killer app for selling stuff

00:25:00.010 --> 00:25:01.103
and it was free.

00:25:02.460 --> 00:25:07.030
So my competitors were printing
catalogs with wine in it

00:25:07.030 --> 00:25:07.890
and shipping it.

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:10.030
Which cost them a lot of
money to make the catalog

00:25:10.030 --> 00:25:12.230
and a lot of money in postage.

00:25:12.230 --> 00:25:14.460
I was gathering people's emails

00:25:14.460 --> 00:25:15.990
every time they came into the store.

00:25:15.990 --> 00:25:16.823
I literally Emma,

00:25:16.823 --> 00:25:18.610
would not let somebody
leave my dad's store

00:25:18.610 --> 00:25:20.280
without giving me their email.

00:25:20.280 --> 00:25:21.703
And I think you can tell my tenacity

00:25:21.703 --> 00:25:23.930
and I was a youngster,
I was real hungry then.

00:25:23.930 --> 00:25:27.410
So and through that, 2 things happened.

00:25:27.410 --> 00:25:29.950
I was faster and it cost me less.

00:25:29.950 --> 00:25:32.490
I was faster because when
Opus one or Chateau Lafitte

00:25:32.490 --> 00:25:35.470
or Dom Perignon's new vintage came out,

00:25:35.470 --> 00:25:38.000
I would email it the day it came in

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:39.420
and I would get the sale.

00:25:39.420 --> 00:25:42.950
A month later, those individuals
would get the catalog

00:25:42.950 --> 00:25:46.700
from my competitors saying
the new Dom Perignon's here.

00:25:46.700 --> 00:25:48.900
So I was winning on speed, but also cost.

00:25:48.900 --> 00:25:52.250
And then finally, the tipping
point was Google AdWords.

00:25:52.250 --> 00:25:55.960
I was smart enough to realize
Google was a big platform,

00:25:55.960 --> 00:25:58.110
they launched the AdWords product.

00:25:58.110 --> 00:26:00.600
And literally on the day they
launched the AdWords product,

00:26:00.600 --> 00:26:02.860
I started buying wine terms.

00:26:02.860 --> 00:26:06.770
At first wine, Bordeaux, Mirlo, Champagne,

00:26:06.770 --> 00:26:09.200
later I had to pick,
you had to get smarter,

00:26:09.200 --> 00:26:10.430
because people started figuring it out.

00:26:10.430 --> 00:26:12.007
But I had about a year there

00:26:12.007 --> 00:26:15.547
where I was paying 5 cents
and then it became 10 cents

00:26:15.547 --> 00:26:17.560
for every wine term you can imagine

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:19.533
and acquiring customers for nothing,

00:26:20.460 --> 00:26:23.010
sometimes 20, 30 cents to get customers,

00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:25.300
active customers, which is insane.

00:26:25.300 --> 00:26:28.490
So that became, about couple years ago,

00:26:28.490 --> 00:26:30.210
I realized what I've
been talking about was

00:26:30.210 --> 00:26:32.920
underpriced attention, meaning,

00:26:32.920 --> 00:26:35.135
the reason I'm so passionate
about LinkedIn now,

00:26:35.135 --> 00:26:38.230
but didn't say a word
about it 6 years ago,

00:26:38.230 --> 00:26:41.270
is the organic reach on a LinkedIn post

00:26:41.270 --> 00:26:43.640
for a business right now is meaningful.

00:26:43.640 --> 00:26:46.740
Because LinkedIn has
so much attention on it

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:51.250
yet not as much content and ads on it yet.

00:26:51.250 --> 00:26:53.050
This is what always happens,

00:26:53.050 --> 00:26:56.850
you're able to get some
organic reach when you post.

00:26:56.850 --> 00:26:58.560
To me that is the holy grail of marketing

00:26:58.560 --> 00:26:59.910
when you're getting people to know about

00:26:59.910 --> 00:27:01.420
your business or you

00:27:01.420 --> 00:27:05.480
and it costs you less aka
either 0 on organic reach

00:27:05.480 --> 00:27:08.830
or why Facebook became so big,

00:27:08.830 --> 00:27:12.030
when you can spend $50
and get people to see it.

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:14.800
One of my issues with
LinkedIn has been that

00:27:14.800 --> 00:27:17.900
there's a floor on the CPM on the cost.

00:27:17.900 --> 00:27:20.430
There's a $2 floor not a 5 cent floor.

00:27:20.430 --> 00:27:23.430
So I want to take advantage
of the long tail of targeting.

00:27:23.430 --> 00:27:26.070
So every time I see my
LinkedIn counterparts

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:28.482
in the US working with
my media, it's funny,

00:27:28.482 --> 00:27:29.900
it's like an ongoing joke,

00:27:29.900 --> 00:27:31.970
because I think you can tell
by the tone I've had here,

00:27:31.970 --> 00:27:35.610
I'm such a fan of LinkedIn but
in one very specific place.

00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:36.443
And I don't understand why you do it,

00:27:36.443 --> 00:27:38.060
because it's a lack of inventory.

00:27:38.060 --> 00:27:40.850
So it allows you to create
a floor, I understand.

00:27:40.850 --> 00:27:42.090
But I still like to razz

00:27:42.090 --> 00:27:44.480
and every time I walked by
a meeting with my media team

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:45.380
and the LinkedIn team,

00:27:45.380 --> 00:27:47.470
I'd pop my head and I'm like,
have you lowered the floor?

00:27:47.470 --> 00:27:49.950
And it's become almost
this like little mean joke.

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:51.950
But that's how I see it,

00:27:51.950 --> 00:27:54.650
which is for certain
things I do not believe

00:27:54.650 --> 00:27:57.240
paying $2 per CPM is the right thing

00:27:57.240 --> 00:27:59.650
and that's why we are
very tactical on LinkedIn.

00:27:59.650 --> 00:28:04.430
In other arenas, paying $70
CPMs is the right thing,

00:28:04.430 --> 00:28:06.370
and we're only paying $13 because

00:28:06.370 --> 00:28:09.236
there's not that much spend
against that narrow thing.

00:28:09.236 --> 00:28:12.037
And that would be an example of me saying,

00:28:12.037 --> 00:28:13.370
"Wow, LinkedIn's remarkable.

00:28:13.370 --> 00:28:18.370
I'm only paying $13 CPMs here
to reach this executive class,

00:28:18.500 --> 00:28:21.140
that's an incredible
deal for this company."

00:28:21.140 --> 00:28:24.800
I'd have to pay, and then
when you amortize conversion,

00:28:24.800 --> 00:28:26.540
I might be paying $13 CPMs.

00:28:26.540 --> 00:28:29.600
But it might cost me
$1,000 to get a customer,

00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:32.790
whereas it will cost me
way more on Facebook,

00:28:32.790 --> 00:28:36.100
or Twitter, or YouTube
to get that same customer

00:28:36.100 --> 00:28:38.990
because LinkedIn has so
much data around people

00:28:38.990 --> 00:28:41.390
and their professional career.

00:28:41.390 --> 00:28:45.780
That's why LinkedIn is an
underpriced medium for me.

00:28:45.780 --> 00:28:48.090
It's media product, its ad product,

00:28:48.090 --> 00:28:50.250
has the capabilities of being better

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:54.640
than anything else in the world
on very specific planning.

00:28:54.640 --> 00:28:56.090
And then on top of that,

00:28:56.090 --> 00:29:00.720
it also has organic reach that I don't see

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:03.200
from anywhere besides TikTok right now

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:06.400
and obviously very 2,
very different audiences.

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:09.440
So underpriced attention
to me is things like

00:29:09.440 --> 00:29:12.580
the Superbowl in America cost $6 million.

00:29:12.580 --> 00:29:15.280
And I think it's unbelievably underpriced

00:29:15.280 --> 00:29:18.630
because 100 million people
will watch your commercial.

00:29:18.630 --> 00:29:20.960
Meanwhile, I think a regular commercial

00:29:20.960 --> 00:29:24.320
on the telly is overpriced,

00:29:24.320 --> 00:29:28.510
because there's assumptions
about GRPs and Nielsen ratings

00:29:28.510 --> 00:29:31.403
that I just know are not
true in common sense.

00:29:32.860 --> 00:29:35.860
Facebook organic used to be the
greatest thing in the world,

00:29:35.860 --> 00:29:39.420
it doesn't even exist
anymore. It's an ad product.

00:29:39.420 --> 00:29:41.630
Running the ads on Facebook as we do

00:29:41.630 --> 00:29:44.290
and we took tune of hundreds
of millions of dollars,

00:29:44.290 --> 00:29:46.370
there's a lot of ways
to make it underpriced.

00:29:46.370 --> 00:29:49.000
It's by doing contextual creative at scale

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:52.810
and doing any different
media spends instead of one.

00:29:52.810 --> 00:29:54.340
There's ways to make it overpriced

00:29:54.340 --> 00:29:55.670
on LinkedIn, YouTube, Facebook,

00:29:55.670 --> 00:29:57.280
where you just do one video

00:29:57.280 --> 00:29:59.740
and you just try to
reach everybody 18 to 55.

00:29:59.740 --> 00:30:03.118
So I spend my life being religious around,

00:30:03.118 --> 00:30:05.290
where's the nook and cranny

00:30:05.290 --> 00:30:07.280
that allows me to pay 5 cents,

00:30:07.280 --> 00:30:10.830
that is really worth 20 cents on business?

00:30:10.830 --> 00:30:12.990
In a minefield where
there's a lot of places

00:30:12.990 --> 00:30:16.300
you can spend 20 cents and
only get 5 cents value.

00:30:16.300 --> 00:30:18.683
That's what I mean by
underpriced attention.

00:30:23.460 --> 00:30:25.130
I can't hear you, Emma.

00:30:25.130 --> 00:30:25.963
- Oh, sorry.

00:30:25.963 --> 00:30:26.796
Can you hear me now, it got better?

00:30:26.796 --> 00:30:28.117
I can, yes it is.

00:30:29.327 --> 00:30:31.393
- No, I just said no,
that was great, thank you.

00:30:31.393 --> 00:30:34.430
So I mean, you obviously over the years,

00:30:34.430 --> 00:30:35.710
you've seen how all the different

00:30:35.710 --> 00:30:37.220
social platforms have evolved,

00:30:37.220 --> 00:30:39.890
you've definitely been
at the cutting edge.

00:30:39.890 --> 00:30:42.220
What do you believe is the
next big thing for social

00:30:42.220 --> 00:30:43.420
or what's coming down the track?

00:30:43.420 --> 00:30:45.680
What do you think is the next big thing?

00:30:45.680 --> 00:30:50.680
- You know what's funny? I don't predict.

00:30:51.250 --> 00:30:53.170
What I'm really great at Emma,

00:30:53.170 --> 00:30:56.803
is being the fastest mover
when it's become obvious.

00:30:58.260 --> 00:31:01.330
So, what I've had a great career on

00:31:01.330 --> 00:31:05.063
is when LinkedIn made that
switch I was there right away.

00:31:06.010 --> 00:31:08.120
I was one of those first 100 influencers

00:31:08.120 --> 00:31:11.423
of that first 100 influencers,
I produced the most content.

00:31:12.420 --> 00:31:15.810
And so, when musically,

00:31:15.810 --> 00:31:19.410
the predecessor to
TikTok had some momentum,

00:31:19.410 --> 00:31:22.100
even though it looked like it
was just 12 year old girls,

00:31:22.100 --> 00:31:24.093
I was in there making content.

00:31:24.960 --> 00:31:28.010
So, I don't know what the next TikTok,

00:31:28.010 --> 00:31:29.420
or Facebook, or LinkedIn,

00:31:29.420 --> 00:31:32.070
LinkedIn and TikTok
are a lot of fun for me

00:31:32.070 --> 00:31:34.660
because they're the 2 leaders of organic,

00:31:34.660 --> 00:31:36.290
which is always the thing I talk about,

00:31:36.290 --> 00:31:39.470
because a lot of people
follow me have no money.

00:31:39.470 --> 00:31:42.270
And so when you have no
money, you need organic.

00:31:42.270 --> 00:31:44.060
What's fun about TikTok and LinkedIn is

00:31:44.060 --> 00:31:46.970
TikTok is the newest of a line of

00:31:46.970 --> 00:31:48.500
Snapchat, Facebook, Twitter,

00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:51.630
a true social network for
the youth that's growing.

00:31:51.630 --> 00:31:55.190
LinkedIn is a great one for
my thesis of not predicting,

00:31:55.190 --> 00:31:57.060
I would have never predicted LinkedIn as

00:31:57.060 --> 00:31:58.990
the next content place.

00:31:58.990 --> 00:32:02.170
LinkedIn is story of a
pivot or not a pivot,

00:32:02.170 --> 00:32:07.170
a very smart addition
to an ongoing platform.

00:32:07.730 --> 00:32:08.980
What that means is,

00:32:08.980 --> 00:32:11.610
one could argue that
YouTube tomorrow can say,

00:32:11.610 --> 00:32:15.600
wait a minute, why don't we
just add a stream of content

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:16.940
that doesn't have to be video

00:32:16.940 --> 00:32:21.468
and they might become a more
meaningful "social network".

00:32:21.468 --> 00:32:24.400
You would probably more
logically argue that,

00:32:24.400 --> 00:32:25.560
that would be a distraction,

00:32:25.560 --> 00:32:27.600
and that's why they haven't done it.

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:32.600
Right now there's 2 incredible
16 year olds in Scotland,

00:32:34.220 --> 00:32:37.270
who might be making the next great app.

00:32:37.270 --> 00:32:38.780
That's not something I could know about,

00:32:38.780 --> 00:32:40.820
I'm not Nostradamus,

00:32:40.820 --> 00:32:43.963
but I can promise you that
if that app gets traction,

00:32:45.290 --> 00:32:46.670
the steam traction,

00:32:46.670 --> 00:32:49.010
because you do see a lot
of apps have a good week,

00:32:49.010 --> 00:32:51.233
or a good month, or a good day.

00:32:52.650 --> 00:32:55.890
And there's an unbelievable
amount of names Peach

00:32:55.890 --> 00:33:00.860
and Vine even had a very
nice run until it was bought,

00:33:00.860 --> 00:33:05.860
Socialcam, Plurk, Friendster, My spot.

00:33:05.990 --> 00:33:08.940
You've got these things that have 1 week,

00:33:08.940 --> 00:33:11.690
even sometimes 3 year runs.

00:33:11.690 --> 00:33:13.060
I'm not a predictor,

00:33:13.060 --> 00:33:16.340
but I'm a practitioner of the moment

00:33:16.340 --> 00:33:17.950
of the apps that are winning.

00:33:22.939 --> 00:33:25.420
(Emma laughs)

00:33:25.420 --> 00:33:26.253
- Brilliant.

00:33:27.880 --> 00:33:29.440
You look at social as a whole

00:33:29.440 --> 00:33:32.400
and if we pivot in a
more tentative VaynerX,

00:33:32.400 --> 00:33:35.013
it's an absolute juggernaut.

00:33:36.063 --> 00:33:38.140
How did you navigate the landscape,

00:33:38.140 --> 00:33:40.763
a landscape a dominated by
global holding companies?

00:33:42.370 --> 00:33:45.140
- Patience, we're 10 years in,

00:33:45.140 --> 00:33:48.610
we're still a $200
million revenue holding,

00:33:48.610 --> 00:33:51.370
which is peanuts, peanuts compared to

00:33:51.370 --> 00:33:54.100
The Publicist and Omnicoms
and things of that nature.

00:33:54.100 --> 00:33:57.150
But we're emerging and we're emerging

00:33:57.150 --> 00:33:59.610
because of underpriced attention.

00:33:59.610 --> 00:34:01.180
What I mean by that is,

00:34:01.180 --> 00:34:04.040
in the same way that I
do underpriced attention,

00:34:04.040 --> 00:34:06.570
as a businessman, I look for opportunities

00:34:06.570 --> 00:34:08.530
that people don't see.

00:34:08.530 --> 00:34:11.050
And we became the best in the world,

00:34:11.050 --> 00:34:13.050
no question and I'm proud to say this

00:34:13.050 --> 00:34:14.440
and I know it sounds audacious,

00:34:14.440 --> 00:34:16.560
but it is very clear to me

00:34:16.560 --> 00:34:20.030
that there is no agency in the world

00:34:20.030 --> 00:34:22.620
that is better at understanding
how to make content

00:34:22.620 --> 00:34:27.000
and run media across the 15 apps

00:34:27.000 --> 00:34:29.050
that dominate the mobile phone.

00:34:29.050 --> 00:34:31.630
LinkedIn, YouTube, Facebook, Twitter.

00:34:31.630 --> 00:34:33.270
And I think people are
starting to realize,

00:34:33.270 --> 00:34:35.520
wait a minute, that is
where brand is being built.

00:34:35.520 --> 00:34:39.000
That is not where you're just
doing lower funnel conversion,

00:34:39.000 --> 00:34:41.882
that's actually where
brand is being built.

00:34:41.882 --> 00:34:43.810
And brand is the game.

00:34:43.810 --> 00:34:46.150
And as the biggest
companies in the world say,

00:34:46.150 --> 00:34:48.364
Oh my God, I don't want
a TV first approach,

00:34:48.364 --> 00:34:51.160
I want a social first approach

00:34:51.160 --> 00:34:54.350
that makes me find what
I should put on TV.

00:34:54.350 --> 00:34:58.960
We be, We've got a loud
leader who's got a decade

00:34:58.960 --> 00:35:01.140
of being historically correct

00:35:01.140 --> 00:35:03.280
on the content that he's put out,

00:35:03.280 --> 00:35:05.230
which gives enormous amount of confidence

00:35:05.230 --> 00:35:06.486
to these decision makers.

00:35:06.486 --> 00:35:10.593
And not to mention the other
thing that I didn't think of,

00:35:10.593 --> 00:35:14.270
because you don't think about
being old when you're young.

00:35:14.270 --> 00:35:19.130
But in a decade or 2 here,
the majority of CMOs,

00:35:20.210 --> 00:35:22.750
that are going to be making the decisions,

00:35:22.750 --> 00:35:25.003
grew up on my content and my books.

00:35:25.970 --> 00:35:28.400
And so just from a brand standpoint

00:35:28.400 --> 00:35:29.810
and we're starting to feel it,

00:35:29.810 --> 00:35:31.270
there's been a couple of
moments here in the last year,

00:35:31.270 --> 00:35:33.800
I'm like, wait a minute this is cool.

00:35:33.800 --> 00:35:36.110
The person that actually
made that decision,

00:35:36.110 --> 00:35:38.580
they decided this was right in university

00:35:38.580 --> 00:35:40.700
when they were reading my book in class,

00:35:40.700 --> 00:35:43.400
like, we don't like,
that was a weird pitch,

00:35:43.400 --> 00:35:45.640
they were saying my words to me,

00:35:45.640 --> 00:35:46.880
we didn't even have to convince them.

00:35:46.880 --> 00:35:49.513
So as the decision maker evolves,

00:35:52.746 --> 00:35:54.850
what we have the benefit of is that

00:35:54.850 --> 00:35:58.800
what we've been focused
on actually became true,

00:35:58.800 --> 00:36:01.480
most of the marketing world dismissed

00:36:01.480 --> 00:36:04.840
and continues, to be frank,
dismisses social media

00:36:04.840 --> 00:36:09.150
or content on these
platforms as a nice to have,

00:36:09.150 --> 00:36:13.140
I view it as the singular source of oxygen

00:36:13.140 --> 00:36:14.913
for businesses going forward.

00:36:16.812 --> 00:36:18.783
You can't worry about anything,

00:36:20.230 --> 00:36:24.210
you have to start with being
incredible content producer,

00:36:24.210 --> 00:36:28.090
to the relevant cohorts and
segments in these channels.

00:36:28.090 --> 00:36:30.150
And then you can worry
about putting your logo

00:36:30.150 --> 00:36:34.240
on a football Jersey
for the Premier League,

00:36:34.240 --> 00:36:36.670
or running an ad on telly,

00:36:36.670 --> 00:36:41.400
or doing outdoor billboards
in the subway systems.

00:36:41.400 --> 00:36:44.390
And because you've already
figured out the right answer

00:36:44.390 --> 00:36:47.560
in these channels at very low cost.

00:36:47.560 --> 00:36:52.560
So we'll see but I've navigated
it by being more right about

00:36:53.450 --> 00:36:55.390
where the world was going.

00:36:55.390 --> 00:36:57.060
And it's not because I'm smarter,

00:36:57.060 --> 00:37:00.210
it's because the holding
companies are attached

00:37:00.210 --> 00:37:01.963
to where the money is right now.

00:37:03.370 --> 00:37:06.040
They're not going to, they're
going to sell commercials

00:37:06.040 --> 00:37:08.290
and because that's where the money is now,

00:37:08.290 --> 00:37:09.530
because they're publicly traded,

00:37:09.530 --> 00:37:11.820
and they have to make
revenue every quarter.

00:37:11.820 --> 00:37:13.890
I'm building something in perpetuity,

00:37:13.890 --> 00:37:16.350
which has allowed me to
play a different game,

00:37:16.350 --> 00:37:18.663
and we're starting to
feel the benefits of that.

00:37:22.950 --> 00:37:26.470
- I'm just gonna pivot slightly
just, I'm conscious of time,

00:37:26.470 --> 00:37:28.450
and there's just one kind of area

00:37:28.450 --> 00:37:30.600
I'd love to get your perspective on.

00:37:30.600 --> 00:37:33.080
Obviously we've talked
about the year we've had,

00:37:33.080 --> 00:37:37.100
I'd love to hear how you
have managed your physical

00:37:37.100 --> 00:37:39.470
and mental well being during the pandemic.

00:37:39.470 --> 00:37:40.900
We were only looking at 2 weeks

00:37:40.900 --> 00:37:42.220
of offices being closed down,

00:37:42.220 --> 00:37:45.070
and then we ended up going
into weeks and then months.

00:37:45.070 --> 00:37:47.610
So I'd love to get how
you manage your physical

00:37:47.610 --> 00:37:49.830
and mental well being

00:37:49.830 --> 00:37:52.200
and how can we kind of create a culture

00:37:52.200 --> 00:37:54.893
of getting people to
prioritize this for themselves?

00:37:56.310 --> 00:38:00.220
- For me, it's been about voices.

00:38:00.220 --> 00:38:02.290
It's recognizing early on that

00:38:02.290 --> 00:38:04.193
this was not gonna be a one day thing.

00:38:05.060 --> 00:38:08.120
And being self aware, this is
a self awareness game, right?

00:38:08.120 --> 00:38:13.120
To me, I know that I do my
best when I am optimistic,

00:38:14.300 --> 00:38:16.370
and when my perspective is proper.

00:38:16.370 --> 00:38:19.237
So I went into a tunnel vision of,

00:38:19.237 --> 00:38:20.688
"Hey, this is a bummer.

00:38:20.688 --> 00:38:23.130
But in the 1940s,

00:38:23.130 --> 00:38:25.210
people were running very
successful companies

00:38:25.210 --> 00:38:27.273
and then the Nazis invaded.

00:38:28.150 --> 00:38:31.220
So this stinks but in the history of time,

00:38:31.220 --> 00:38:32.320
there's been tougher times."

00:38:32.320 --> 00:38:34.070
And for me that always works,

00:38:34.070 --> 00:38:36.050
for me to lean into gratitude,

00:38:36.050 --> 00:38:39.123
and into worst case scenario
that works for my psyche.

00:38:40.360 --> 00:38:44.730
I also have had enough
success in my career,

00:38:44.730 --> 00:38:45.620
that's 6 years ago,

00:38:45.620 --> 00:38:49.350
I was able to afford a full
time trainer, just for me.

00:38:49.350 --> 00:38:53.950
And that is how I got my health
into a much better place.

00:38:53.950 --> 00:38:58.150
And so I knew that by
not him "babysitting me"

00:38:58.150 --> 00:38:59.100
that I was vulnerable.

00:38:59.100 --> 00:39:01.057
So I had a very quick call
with him early on and said,

00:39:01.057 --> 00:39:03.383
"Hey, we're gonna train virtually on zoom,

00:39:03.383 --> 00:39:05.930
you're going to watch me
go on the scale every day,

00:39:05.930 --> 00:39:07.347
so I don't lose my way."

00:39:08.190 --> 00:39:12.720
And so I kind of attacked
a strategy early on

00:39:12.720 --> 00:39:14.317
from a physical and mental place.

00:39:14.317 --> 00:39:16.940
And I think everybody
here needs to do the same.

00:39:16.940 --> 00:39:20.640
If that you binge eat when you're stressed

00:39:20.640 --> 00:39:24.050
and you don't wanna put
on 30 pounds during COVID.

00:39:24.050 --> 00:39:27.110
You need to first acknowledge that truth

00:39:27.110 --> 00:39:29.840
and then try to find a
system that works for you,

00:39:29.840 --> 00:39:32.930
whether that's asking your
spouse to hold you accountable,

00:39:32.930 --> 00:39:35.170
whether that's creating a Facebook group,

00:39:35.170 --> 00:39:37.170
whether that's putting out content

00:39:37.170 --> 00:39:39.090
and holding yourself accountable,

00:39:39.090 --> 00:39:42.350
whether that's throwing
out all the food that's,

00:39:42.350 --> 00:39:44.660
my mom and I are very similar,

00:39:44.660 --> 00:39:46.880
we'll eat literally
anything in front of us.

00:39:46.880 --> 00:39:49.780
So if it's a candy bar
or if it's green beans,

00:39:49.780 --> 00:39:51.220
it's all the same, we're just,

00:39:51.220 --> 00:39:53.350
we call ourselves lawn mowers.

00:39:53.350 --> 00:39:57.110
I have successfully taken
all bad food out of my house.

00:39:57.110 --> 00:39:58.910
My mom continues to not

00:39:58.910 --> 00:40:01.830
and thus she's had a
different experience in COVID

00:40:01.830 --> 00:40:03.620
than I have had.

00:40:03.620 --> 00:40:05.830
I think it's self awareness,

00:40:05.830 --> 00:40:09.140
and but then also not over
judging yourself, Emma.

00:40:09.140 --> 00:40:11.350
I think the reason people struggle

00:40:11.350 --> 00:40:13.073
is they beat themselves up.

00:40:14.625 --> 00:40:17.250
And I'd like everybody to
have a little more compassion

00:40:17.250 --> 00:40:20.110
and sympathy for themselves
during this time,

00:40:20.110 --> 00:40:25.110
aka, if you did gain 15 pounds
in this year, it's okay,

00:40:25.130 --> 00:40:26.220
when we get back to normal

00:40:26.220 --> 00:40:28.790
and you can go and do what works for you.

00:40:28.790 --> 00:40:30.510
In the next year you'll lose that

00:40:30.510 --> 00:40:33.770
and so enjoy the candy while you can.

00:40:33.770 --> 00:40:37.953
So I think it's a balance
of strategy and self love.

00:40:40.970 --> 00:40:42.420
- Really great advice there as well.

00:40:42.420 --> 00:40:44.840
I think it's been a tough year for people.

00:40:44.840 --> 00:40:47.060
So that's really great to hear.

00:40:47.060 --> 00:40:48.880
- Yeah, it's been a very challenging year.

00:40:48.880 --> 00:40:50.910
And I think a couple things.

00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:54.160
1. why should we as humans

00:40:54.160 --> 00:40:57.370
live lives during all easy times

00:40:57.370 --> 00:40:59.130
when our grandparents, great grandparents,

00:40:59.130 --> 00:41:01.000
great, great grandparents didn't?

00:41:01.000 --> 00:41:03.483
So I think one, the first
thing everyone has to do is

00:41:03.483 --> 00:41:07.210
get over their own audacity

00:41:07.210 --> 00:41:09.950
and entitlement to like this great,

00:41:09.950 --> 00:41:11.770
it's not the way it actually works,

00:41:11.770 --> 00:41:13.510
you have both sides of the coin.

00:41:13.510 --> 00:41:15.130
I think once you get over that,

00:41:15.130 --> 00:41:17.710
if you don't beat yourself up for not,

00:41:17.710 --> 00:41:19.020
A lot of people beating themselves up

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:19.970
because they didn't save money,

00:41:19.970 --> 00:41:22.940
even though they knew it
was the right thing to do,

00:41:22.940 --> 00:41:25.340
they didn't because they wanted
to live a certain lifestyle

00:41:25.340 --> 00:41:27.930
and now they are in a pickle.

00:41:27.930 --> 00:41:29.860
What many will do in that scenario

00:41:29.860 --> 00:41:33.030
is start to really be
upset with themselves.

00:41:33.030 --> 00:41:35.850
You should have a self reflection moment.

00:41:35.850 --> 00:41:37.490
But you can't go into a time machine

00:41:37.490 --> 00:41:38.470
and change the fact that

00:41:38.470 --> 00:41:40.170
you went on vacations you couldn't afford

00:41:40.170 --> 00:41:42.490
and buy clothes you couldn't afford.

00:41:42.490 --> 00:41:43.473
So you're not gonna be able to do that,

00:41:43.473 --> 00:41:45.680
what you can do is make
a commitment to yourself

00:41:45.680 --> 00:41:48.550
to going forward acting
in a different way.

00:41:48.550 --> 00:41:50.490
And I hope that people learn

00:41:50.490 --> 00:41:52.190
about themselves during this time.

